# Stabilising Continuous Authentication Systems Using Telecom-Inspired Hysteresis-Based Decision Logic

Name: [Your Name]
Student ID: [Your Student ID]
Word Count: ~4900

## Abstract

This paper investigates how research design choices influence the quality, validity, and real-world impact of Computer Science research, using continuous authentication systems as a case study.

## Introduction

Continuous authentication has emerged as a promising approach for enhancing security in mobile and ubiquitous computing environments.

## Problem Statement

Despite advances in machine learning-based authentication, practical deployments continue to suffer from decision instability.

## Aim

The aim of this research is to investigate whether telecom-inspired hysteresis-based decision mechanisms can stabilise continuous authentication systems.

## Objectives

1. Analyse instability in continuous authentication decisions.
2. Design a hysteresis-based decision framework.
3. Evaluate stability using temporal metrics.

## Research Questions

RQ1: How do decision-level design choices affect authentication stability?
RQ2: Can hysteresis reduce ping-pong effects without sacrificing accuracy?

## Justification

This research is justified by the growing deployment of context-aware authentication systems.

## Literature Review

Recent studies in continuous authentication focus heavily on classifier accuracy.

## Methodology

This study adopts an experimental research design using real-world sensor data.

## Conclusion

This research demonstrates that instability in continuous authentication is often a design failure rather than a modelling failure.

## References

References will be formatted in APA (7th edition) and limited to works published within the past five years.